description:
## C++
`Pathfinder::getAircraftPath` (`GeneralsMD/Code/GameEngine/Source/GameLogic/AI/AIPathfind.cpp:5781-5846`):
```
Coord3D pos = *obj->getPosition();
pos.z = to->z;
thePath->prependNode(&pos, LAYER_GROUND);
```
The first node is the unit XY at **destination altitude**. Detour inserts from `segmentIntersectsTallBuilding` keep the radial-offset positions (not flattened back to start Z). Last node is `adjDest` (also dest Z).

## Leftover
`PathfindingSystem::get_aircraft_path` (`GeneralsRust/Code/GameEngine/GameLogic/src/ai/pathfind_complete/line_passable.rs:390-462`):
```
let mut start = *from;
start.z = to.z;
let mut waypoints = vec![start, adj_dest];
```
Test `get_aircraft_path_two_node_baseline` asserts `waypoints[0].z == to.z`. Detours insert n1/n2/n3 without rewriting start Z.

## Live
`PathfindingSystem::find_path_ex_surfaces` (`GeneralsRust/Code/Main/src/game_logic/pathfinding.rs:6656-6667`):
```
if aircraft {
    for p in path.iter_mut() { p.y = start.y; }
    if let Some(last) = path.last_mut() { last.y = goal.y; }
}
if let Some(first) = path.first_mut() { *first = start; }
```
Host Y-up: every aircraft node (including first and tall-building detours) is forced to **current altitude**, then first is overwritten to the raw start pose. Only the last waypoint keeps dest Y.

## Player-visible
Jets/helis/Chinooks do not immediately fly at dest altitude. They stay at current height until the last node, so climb/dive is deferred to arrival instead of C++/leftover dest-Z first node.

## Evidence
C++ `AIPathfind.cpp:5809-5811`; leftover `line_passable.rs:405-407` + `pathfind_complete/tests.rs` `get_aircraft_path_two_node_baseline`; live `pathfinding.rs:6656-6667`. Not a duplicate of closed hq-8vdsw (WINGS clip gate) or hq-2pdvs (building set/offset).

## C++
`LaserUpdate::initLaser` (`LaserUpdate.cpp:347-366`) always writes the **drawable** position after resolving start/end:

```
//Important! Set the laser position to the average of both points or else
//it probably won't get rendered!!!
// And as a client update, we cannot set the logic position.
if (parent == NULL) { midpoint(startPos, endPos); }
else { parent->getPosition(); }
draw->setPosition(&posToUse);
```

Weapon.cpp:2431-2432 and AssistedTargetingUpdate.cpp:104-105 also `laser->setPosition(source)` first so frustum culling has a real world point. PUC `newDrawable` then `initLaser(NULL, NULL, start, end)` relies on the midpoint write.

## Rust
Live `LaserUpdate::init_laser` (`object/update/laser_update.rs:448-504`) records start/end and particles, then `self.dirty = true`. It never calls `drawable.set_position` (parent or midpoint).

Leftover `TheGameClient::create_drawable` (`helpers/game_client.rs:416-543`) inserts `DrawableState.position = Coord3D::ZERO`. `init_drawable_laser` (`:639-677`) always calls `init_laser(None, None, start, end, "", growth)` — the C++ parent==NULL branch that **must** midpoint — but only stamps `beam_start`/`beam_end` on DRAWABLE_STATE. PUC leftover (`particle_uplink_cannon_update.rs:1076-1234`, `:1564`) is exactly this parentless path.

## Evidence
C++ LaserUpdate.cpp:347-366 (comment + setPosition).
Rust `laser_update.rs:448-504` (no set_position); `helpers/game_client.rs:530, 639-677`; PUC `create_drawable` + `init_drawable_laser`.

## Player-visible effect
Parentless beams (Particle Uplink Cannon connector / ground-to-orbit / orbit-to-target, any `init_drawable_laser` caller) stay at world origin. C++ warns they will not render. Camera on the map sees missing or popping PUC/special-ability lasers until something else moves the drawable.

## C++
`Particle::Particle` (`ParticleSys.cpp:269-283`) always seeds runtime state from key 0:

```
m_alpha = m_alphaKey[0].value;
m_alphaTargetKey = 1;
computeAlphaRate();
m_color = m_colorKey[0].color;
m_colorTargetKey = 1;
computeColorRate();
```

`Alpha1`/`Color1` are authored as `low high frame` / `R: G: B: frame` (`parseRandomKeyframe` / `parseRGBColorKeyframe` at `:2683-2706`). Retail first keys use **frame 0**. Frame 0 on later keys means “unused”; on key 0 it is the start pose. Unset Color1 stays `{0,0,0}` so C++ newborns start black.

## Rust
Live `Particle::new` (`GameClient/src/effects/particle_system.rs:158-189`) defaults `alpha = 1.0`, `color = [1,1,1]`, then:

```
if particle.alpha_keys[0].frame > 0 { apply key 0 }
if particle.color_keys[0].frame > 0 { apply key 0 }
```

So the normal INI case (frame 0) is skipped. Leftover `Main/src/effects/particle_system.rs:381-383` never uses keyframes at all (linear fade by lifetime).

## Evidence
C++ ctor applies key[0] with no frame test. Live ctor’s `frame > 0` guard plus white/opaque defaults. `generate_particle_info` (`:2038-2047`) does copy sampled key 0 into `ParticleInfo`; the drop happens at `Particle::new`.

## Player-visible
Fade-in smoke/fire starts fully opaque; colored bursts (muzzle, explosion, jet exhaust) flash white until the second keyframe, then snap. Missing Color1 templates stay white instead of black.

## C++
`GameEngine::execute` (`GameEngine.cpp:768-867`) stores `prevTime` before the loop (then at end of each wait). After `update()` (which includes `TheGameClient->UPDATE()` → `W3DDisplay::draw` and its own `minTime=30` busy-wait gated on `m_useFpsLimit`), it waits:

```
DWORD limit = (1000.0f/m_maxFPS)-1;
while (TheGlobalData->m_useFpsLimit && (now - prevTime) < limit) {
    Sleep(0);
    now = timeGetTime();
}
prevTime = now;
```

`(now - prevTime)` **includes** the just-finished `update()`+draw. If the frame already took ≥ limit (or ≥ W3D 30 ms inside draw), execute does not wait extra. Effective period is max(work, limit), not work+limit.

`getTimeMultiplier()<=1 && !isTimeFast()` gates the whole throttle; TiVO+replay skips it.

## Rust
Live windowed `AboutToWait` (`run_loop.rs:810-817`) always:

```
drive_frame(...); // update + render (W3D analog already inside)
next_redraw_at = Instant::now() + engine.live_present_interval().unwrap_or(ZERO);
```

`live_present_interval` (`run_loop.rs:927-947`) returns `max(exec_ms, draw_ms)` ≈ 29 ms when `use_fps_limit` (exec `(1000/maxFPS)-1`, draw `30-1`). That duration is added **after** `drive_frame` returns.

hq-nzr3 closed the 60→45 constant. This remaining gap is the wait *placement*: C++ period includes work; live WaitUntil is extra idle after work.

## Evidence
```
// GameEngine.cpp:856-867 — wait measured from prevTime (previous wait end / this iteration start)
DWORD now = timeGetTime();
DWORD limit = (1000.0f/m_maxFPS)-1;
while (TheGlobalData->m_useFpsLimit && (now - prevTime) < limit) { Sleep(0); now = timeGetTime(); }
prevTime = now;
```

```
// run_loop.rs:814-817
drive_frame(engine, current_window, &mut runtime_host_bridge, true);
current_window.request_redraw();
next_redraw_at = Instant::now()
    + engine.live_present_interval().unwrap_or(Duration::ZERO);
```

## Player-visible effect
A 40 ms update+render frame is ~40 ms in C++ (limit already satisfied) but ~40+29 ms (~14–17 FPS) on the live host. Slow frames (shell map, model load) feel half-speed versus retail. Unrelated to the already-closed 45 vs 60 cap constant.

## C++
`GameEngine::init` (`GameEngine.cpp:360-361`) constructs `TheGameLODManager` after `parseCommandLine` and calls `TheGameLODManager->init()` before Water/Weather INI.

`GameLODManager::init` (`GameLOD.cpp:244-339`) loads `Data\INI\GameLOD.ini` + `GameLODPresets.ini`, snapshots Custom from GlobalData, reads `OptionPreferences` (`Options.ini`), and `setStaticLODLevel(userSetDetail)`. Custom detail copies TextureReduction, 3D/2D shadows, building occlusion, particle cap, dynamic LOD, **UseFPSLimit**, lightmap/cloud/water/heat, extra animations, trees.

`GameEngine.cpp:535` then `setFramesPerSecondLimit(TheGlobalData->m_framesPerSecondLimit)` so the execute cap matches the options-applied GlobalData.

