## C++
`ThingTemplate.cpp:129` `{ "DisplayColor", INI::parseColorInt, offsetof(m_displayColor) }`.
`INI.cpp:1032-1071` `parseColorInt` reads colon-separated labeled tokens `R:<0-255> G:<0-255> B:<0-255>` with optional `A:<0-255>` (A defaults to 255) via `getSepsColon()`, then `GameMakeColor`. Constructor does not initialize `m_displayColor` (pool/default-template typically leaves it unset / GAME_COLOR_UNDEFINED `0x00FFFFFF`).

## Rust
`thing_template.rs:3045-3048` `DisplayColor` uses `parse_color_int`, which (`3472-3475`) parses a single hex RRGGBB token (`0x` optional) and forces alpha `0xFF`. Retail `R:255 G:128 B:0` fails `from_str_radix`, the `if let Ok` swallows the error, and the field stays `Color::white()` (`0xFFFFFFFF`, `thing_template.rs:1623`).

## Evidence
```
// C++ INI.cpp:1034-1071
const char* names[4] = { "R", "G", "B", "A" };
// token must match names[i], then scanInt; A optional default 255
*theColor = GameMakeColor(colors[0], colors[1], colors[2], colors[3]);
```
```
// Rust thing_template.rs:3472-3475
fn parse_color_int(s: &str) -> Result<Color, ()> {
    let v = u32::from_str_radix(s.trim_start_matches("0x"), 16).map_err(|_| ())?;
    Ok(Color(0xFF000000 | v))
}
```

## Player-visible effect
World Builder / editor object tint and any UI that reads `getDisplayColor()` stay white instead of the authored RGB. Not a combat path, but the INI key is mis-parsed (wrong units/format).

## Split hint
Reuse the shared `parseColorInt` / `GameMakeColor` helper already used by DrawGroupInfo / OnlineChatColors.

